{"timestamp": "2026-01-16 22:47:55", "level": "DEBUG", "module": "anthropic._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': Timeout(connect=5.0, read=600, write=600, pool=600), 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-36e04e44-cab2-4efb-8ad6-6ec18d47b072', 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4096, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Please complete the following task:\n\n{\n  "requirements_file": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/recovery_visibility/requirements.md",\n  "output_file": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md",\n  "project_name": "Recovery Visibility Implementation"\n}\n\nRemember to respond with valid JSON matching the expected output format.\n'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_01Saqis42ZCJEg9umEsBKxDc', 'name': 'read_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/recovery_visibility/requirements.md'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_01Saqis42ZCJEg9umEsBKxDc', 'content': '{"success": true, "content": "# Recovery Dashboard Visibility & Stateful Persistence\\n\\n## Problem Statement\\n1. **Recovery Visibility**: Recovered agents don\'t appear in the dashboard as first-class citizens\\n2. **State Loss**: Process restarts or memory issues cause loss of all swarm state\\n3. **No Historical Context**: Cannot see what prompts were submitted, their status, or results\\n\\n## Current State Analysis\\n\\n### What We Have\\n- `~/.ensemble/projects/` contains project tracking JSON files\\n- Activity tracker stores agent states in memory only\\n- Recovery system can restart failed agents but they don\'t integrate with UI\\n- Generated markdown files contain some context but aren\'t queryable\\n\\n### What We\'re Missing\\n- Persistent storage of complete swarm state\\n- Dashboard integration for recovered agents\\n- Prompt history and status tracking\\n- Automatic state reload on backend restart\\n\\n## Requirements\\n\\n### Part 1: Persistent Swarm State\\n\\n#### 1.1 Data Model\\nStore the following for each swarm session:\\n\\n```python\\nSwarmSession:\\n  session_id: str           # UUID\\n  started_at: datetime\\n  prompt: str               # Original user prompt\\n  budget_tier: str          # economical/balanced/full_firepower\\n  status: str               # running/completed/failed/paused\\n  project_id: str           # Link to project tracking\\n  family_name: str          # Agent family (new feature)\\n\\n  # Metrics\\n  total_cost: float\\n  total_tokens: int\\n  duration_ms: int\\n\\n  # Agent tracking\\n  agents: Dict[str, AgentState]\\n  hierarchy: Dict           # Parent-child relationships\\n\\n  # Results\\n  deliverables: List[str]   # Generated file paths\\n  summary: str              # Final summary\\n  errors: List[str]         # Any errors encountered\\n\\nAgentState:\\n  agent_id: str\\n  agent_type: str\\n  whimsical_name: str\\n  family_name: str\\n  status: str               # running/completed/failed\\n  started_at: datetime\\n  completed_at: datetime\\n  iterations: int\\n  max_iterations: int\\n  model_used: str\\n  cost: float\\n  input_tokens: int\\n  output_tokens: int\\n  current_task: str\\n  summary: str\\n  self_analysis: str\\n  parent_id: str            # For hierarchy reconstruction\\n```\\n\\n#### 1.2 Storage Backend\\n- Use SQLite for persistent storage (already have agent_metrics.db)\\n- Tables: `swarm_sessions`, `agent_states`, `activities`\\n- JSON fields for complex nested data\\n- Automatic schema migrations\\n\\n#### 1.3 Persistence Points\\n- Save state on:\\n  - Agent spawn\\n  - Agent completion/failure\\n  - Iteration milestone (every 5 iterations)\\n  - Tool use completion\\n  - Question asked/answered\\n  - Process shutdown (graceful)\\n\\n### Part 2: Recovery Dashboard Integration\\n\\n#### 2.1 On Backend Startup\\n1. Load all incomplete sessions from database\\n2. Reconstruct agent states into memory\\n3. Mark sessions as \\"recovered\\" with original state preserved\\n4. Display recovered agents in dashboard immediately\\n\\n#### 2.2 Recovered Agent Display\\n- Show with special indicator (e.g., \\"Recovered\\" badge)\\n- Display original prompt and context\\n- Show what was completed vs pending\\n- Allow user to:\\n  - Resume execution\\n  - Mark as abandoned\\n  - View full history\\n\\n#### 2.3 API Endpoints\\n```\\nGET  /api/sessions                    # List all sessions (active + historical)\\nGET  /api/sessions/{id}               # Get session details\\nGET  /api/sessions/{id}/agents        # Get agents for session\\nPOST /api/sessions/{id}/resume        # Resume recovered session\\nPOST /api/sessions/{id}/abandon       # Mark session as abandoned\\n\\nGET  /api/history                     # Paginated prompt history\\nGET  /api/history/{prompt_id}/status  # Status of specific prompt\\n```\\n\\n### Part 3: Dashboard UI Changes\\n\\n#### 3.1 Session List View\\nAdd a \\"Sessions\\" or \\"History\\" tab showing:\\n- All swarm sessions (current and historical)\\n- Status indicator (running/completed/failed/recovered)\\n- Original prompt (truncated)\\n- Start time and duration\\n- Cost and agent count\\n- Click to expand details\\n\\n#### 3.2 Recovered Session Card\\n```\\n\\u250c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2510\\n\\u2502 \\ud83d\\udd04 RECOVERED SESSION                       \\u2502\\n\\u2502 Prompt: \\"Implement token tracking...\\"      \\u2502\\n\\u2502 Started: 2 hours ago | Cost: $0.45        \\u2502\\n\\u2502 Progress: 3/5 agents completed            \\u2502\\n\\u2502                                           \\u2502\\n\\u2502 [Resume] [View Details] [Abandon]         \\u2502\\n\\u2514\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2518\\n```\\n\\n#### 3.3 Status Indicators\\n- \\ud83d\\udfe2 Running - Active execution\\n- \\u2705 Completed - Successfully finished\\n- \\u274c Failed - Execution failed\\n- \\ud83d\\udd04 Recovered - Loaded from persistence, needs action\\n- \\u23f8\\ufe0f Paused - Waiting for user input\\n\\n### Part 4: Graceful Shutdown\\n\\n#### 4.1 Shutdown Procedure\\n1. Detect SIGTERM/SIGINT signals\\n2. Mark all running agents as \\"interrupted\\"\\n3. Save current state to database\\n4. Complete any pending database writes\\n5. Exit cleanly\\n\\n#### 4.2 Crash Recovery\\n- Database transactions ensure atomic writes\\n- On crash, last persisted state is recovered\\n- Some in-flight operations may be lost (acceptable)\\n\\n## Implementation Plan\\n\\n### Phase 1: Database Schema (Backend)\\n1. Create SQLite tables for sessions and agent states\\n2. Add persistence hooks to ActivityTracker\\n3. Implement save/load functions\\n4. Add graceful shutdown handling\\n\\n### Phase 2: Recovery Integration (Backend)\\n1. Load state on backend startup\\n2. Reconstruct agent hierarchy\\n3. Mark recovered sessions appropriately\\n4. Add API endpoints for session management\\n\\n### Phase 3: Dashboard Integration (Frontend)\\n1. Add sessions/history view\\n2. Display recovered sessions\\n3. Implement resume/abandon actions\\n4. Add status indicators throughout UI\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n1. Backend restart preserves all swarm state\\n2. Recovered agents appear in dashboard immediately after restart\\n3. Users can resume or abandon recovered sessions\\n4. Full prompt history accessible via UI\\n5. No data loss on graceful shutdown\\n6. Crash recovery restores last known state\\n7. Performance impact minimal (<100ms startup overhead)\\n\\n## Files to Modify/Create\\n\\n### New Files\\n- `src/runtime/agents/persistence.py` - State persistence layer\\n- `src/runtime/agents/session_manager.py` - Session lifecycle management\\n- Database migration scripts\\n\\n### Modified Files\\n- `src/runtime/agents/activity_tracker.py` - Add persistence hooks\\n- `src/field/ensemble_ui/backend/main.py` - Add API endpoints, startup recovery\\n- Frontend components for session display\\n\\n## Out of Scope\\n- Distributed state (multi-node persistence)\\n- Real-time sync between instances\\n- State export/import functionality\\n"}'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_014QW3r6cvf4JyTL8Kkw61GT', 'name': 'write_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md', 'content': '# Recovery Visibility Architecture Proposal\n\n## 1. Architecture Overview\n\n### System Purpose\nDesign a robust, stateful system for tracking and recovering AI agent swarm executions with persistent state management and comprehensive visibility.\n\n### Architecture Pattern\n**Layered Hexagonal Architecture** with clear separation of concerns:\n- Persistence Layer (Database)\n- State Management Layer\n- API Layer\n- UI Layer\n\n## 2. Tech Stack\n\n### Backend\n- **Language**: Python 3.10+\n- **Database**: SQLite (with SQLAlchemy ORM)\n- **Web Framework**: FastAPI\n- **State Management**: Pydantic models\n- **Background Processing**: Asyncio\n\n### Frontend\n- **Framework**: React\n- **State Management**: Redux\n- **UI Library**: Chakra UI\n- **HTTP Client**: Axios\n\n### Tools\n- **CI/CD**: GitHub Actions\n- **Testing**: pytest (backend), Jest (frontend)\n- **Containerization**: Docker\n\n## 3. System Components\n\n### Backend Components\n1. **Persistence Manager**\n   - Responsible for SQLite database interactions\n   - Handles state serialization/deserialization\n   - Manages database migrations\n\n2. **Session Tracker**\n   - Tracks overall swarm session lifecycle\n   - Manages agent state reconstruction\n   - Handles recovery and resumption logic\n\n3. **API Controller**\n   - Exposes RESTful endpoints for session management\n   - Handles session listing, retrieval, and actions\n   - Implements authentication and authorization\n\n### Frontend Components\n1. **Sessions Dashboard**\n   - Displays current and historical sessions\n   - Shows recovery status and actions\n   - Provides detailed session exploration\n\n2. **Session Detail View**\n   - Renders comprehensive session information\n   - Displays agent hierarchy and individual agent states\n   - Supports resume/abandon actions\n\n## 4. Data Flow\n\n```\nUser Input → API Controller → Session Tracker → Persistence Manager\nPersistence Manager → Session Tracker → API Controller → Frontend\n```\n\n## 5. Database Schema\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE swarm_sessions (\n    session_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n    prompt TEXT,\n    status TEXT,\n    started_at DATETIME,\n    completed_at DATETIME,\n    total_cost REAL,\n    total_tokens INTEGER,\n    family_name TEXT\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE agent_states (\n    agent_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n    session_id TEXT,\n    agent_type TEXT,\n    status TEXT,\n    started_at DATETIME,\n    completed_at DATETIME,\n    iterations INTEGER,\n    model_used TEXT,\n    FOREIGN KEY(session_id) REFERENCES swarm_sessions(session_id)\n);\n```\n\n## 6. API Design\n\n### Session Endpoints\n- `GET /api/sessions` - List all sessions\n- `GET /api/sessions/{id}` - Get session details\n- `POST /api/sessions/{id}/resume` - Resume session\n- `POST /api/sessions/{id}/abandon` - Abandon session\n\n## 7. Deployment Strategy\n- Containerized deployment using Docker\n- CI/CD pipeline with GitHub Actions\n- Automated database migrations\n- Minimal downtime during updates\n\n## 8. Testing Strategy\n- Unit tests for each component\n- Integration tests for API endpoints\n- State reconstruction tests\n- Persistence scenario testing\n\n## 9. Risks and Mitigations\n1. **Data Corruption**\n   - Mitigation: Use SQLite transactions\n   - Implement robust error handling\n   - Regular state checksums\n\n2. **Performance Overhead**\n   - Mitigation: Optimize database queries\n   - Implement efficient serialization\n   - Limit historical data retention\n\n## 10. Open Questions\n- Long-term storage strategy for historical sessions\n- Potential read-replica for large-scale deployments\n\n## 11. Key Trade-offs\n- SQLite vs distributed database\n- In-memory caching strategies\n- Performance vs comprehensive tracking\n\n## Conclusion\nA robust, flexible architecture enabling comprehensive session tracking and recovery with minimal complexity and performance impact.'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_014QW3r6cvf4JyTL8Kkw61GT', 'content': '{"success": true, "message": "File written successfully to /Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md (backup: /Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture_20260116_224746.md)", "backup_path": "/Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture_20260116_224746.md", "was_overwrite": true}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-3-5-haiku-20241022', 'system': 'You are a System Architect agent.\n\n## Purpose\nDesigns the show formations and execution strategy. Designs software architecture based on requirements documents. Proposes tech stack, system structure, component breakdown, and deployment approach. Creates architecture proposals that require user approval before implementation begins.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are a software architect. Your job is to design a robust, maintainable architecture based on requirements. Your proposal will be reviewed by the user before implementation begins.\n\n### Your Process:\n\n**BE DECISIVE**: Make architectural decisions based on industry best practices. Only escalate when major trade-offs exist (e.g., SQL vs NoSQL for specific use case with conflicting requirements).\n\n**Default Choices** (use unless requirements specify otherwise):\n**Web Apps**: React/Vue frontend + Python/Node backend + PostgreSQL\n**APIs**: REST with OpenAPI docs, JWT auth\n**Deployment**: Docker containers + cloud platform\n**Testing**: Jest/pytest, CI/CD with GitHub Actions\n**State**: Redux/Vuex for complex UIs, Context API for simple\n\n1. **Read Requirements**: Use read_file to thoroughly understand the requirements document\n\n2. **Analyze Constraints**: Consider:\nFunctional requirements (what must it do?)\nNon-functional requirements (performance, scale, security)\nTechnical constraints (languages, platforms, existing systems)\nResource constraints (time, complexity, cost)\n\n3. **Design Architecture**: Create a comprehensive proposal with these sections:\n\n   **a) Architecture Overview**\nHigh-level system design\nArchitecture pattern (e.g., MVC, microservices, layered)\nRationale for chosen approach\n\n   **b) Tech Stack**\nLanguages and frameworks\nLibraries and dependencies\nTools and platforms\n**For each choice, explain WHY and what alternatives were considered**\n\n   **c) System Components**\nComponent breakdown\nResponsibility of each component\nHow components interact\nData flow diagrams (in text/markdown)\n\n   **d) File/Directory Structure**\nProposed directory layout\nModule organization\nConfiguration file locations\n\n   **e) Data Model** (if applicable)\nDatabase schema\nData structures\nState management approach\n\n   **f) API Design** (if applicable)\nEndpoint structure\nRequest/response formats\nAuthentication approach\n\n   **g) Deployment Strategy**\nHow will this be deployed?\nEnvironment configuration\nCI/CD considerations\n\n   **h) Testing Strategy**\nUnit testing approach\nIntegration testing approach\nHow to verify requirements are met\n\n   **i) Alternatives Considered**\nWhat other approaches were considered?\nWhy were they rejected?\nTrade-offs of chosen approach\n\n   **j) Risks and Mitigations**\nPotential risks in this architecture\nHow to mitigate them\n\n   **k) Open Questions**\nDecisions that need user input\nTrade-offs where user preference matters\n\n4. **Write the Proposal**: Use write_file to create a clear, comprehensive markdown document\n\n5. **Return Key Decisions**: Highlight the most important architectural decisions for user review\n\n### Guidelines:\n**Justify all decisions** - don\'t just choose tech, explain why\n**Consider alternatives** - show you\'ve thought through options\n**Be specific** - provide concrete details, not just buzzwords\n**Think about maintainability** - code will need to be updated\n**Consider the full lifecycle** - development, testing, deployment, monitoring\n**Match complexity to need** - don\'t over-engineer, but don\'t under-design\n**Highlight trade-offs** - be honest about pros and cons\n**Make it reviewable** - user needs to understand and approve this\n\n## Input Format\nYour input will be provided in the following format:\n```json\n{\n  "requirements_file": "Path to requirements document to base architecture on",\n  "output_file": "Path where architecture proposal should be written",\n  "constraints": "Optional: Specific technical constraints (e.g., must use Python, deploy on AWS)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\nYou must respond with JSON matching this exact format:\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|needs_clarification",\n  "architecture_file": "Path to the written architecture proposal",\n  "message": "Summary of architectural approach",\n  "key_decisions": ["List of major architectural decisions for user review"],\n  "self_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Your performance analysis (2-4 sentences, see Self-Improvement Directive)"\n}\n```\n\n## When to Request Clarification\nRequest clarification when:\n\n\n## Termination Conditions\nYou are done when:\n- Complete architecture proposal has been written\n- Tech stack is justified with trade-offs explained\n- File structure and component breakdown are defined\n- Data flow is documented\n- Alternative approaches are considered\n\nCRITICAL: Your response must be valid JSON matching the Output Format exactly.\n\n## Your Performance History\n- Success rate: 96.9%\n- Average iterations per task: 3.4\n- Common errors to avoid: RateLimitError, CircuitBreakerOpenError\n\n**Tips for this run:**\n- Watch out for these common errors: RateLimitError, CircuitBreakerOpenError\n- Great work! Maintain your high performance standards.\n\n**Learnings from your recent runs:**\n- The architecture proposal successfully addresses the complex requirements of agent pipeline continuation while maintaining simplicity and performance. By focusing on async processing and clear state m...\n- The architecture proposal successfully translates the milestone plan into a robust, extensible system design. By breaking down each bot\'s responsibilities and establishing clear interfaces, the design...\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'write_file', 'description': 'Write content to a file, creating parent directories if needed. Automatically backs up existing files.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to write'}, 'content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Content to write to the file'}}, 'required': ['file_path', 'content']}}, {'name': 'read_file', 'description': 'Read content from a file', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to read'}}, 'required': ['file_path']}}, {'name': 'run_command', 'description': 'Execute a shell command and return the output', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The shell com